WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.479 --> 00:00:08.250
The Arctic Circle is the line above which
you get midnight sun at least one day a year.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:12.217
And also where you get one day where the sun
doesn't peek above the horizon at all.

00:00:12.217 --> 00:00:15.185
But despite its reputation
for being cold and northern,

00:00:15.185 --> 00:00:18.350
Iceland is not above the Arctic Circle.

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:19.900
Well, most of it isn't.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:26.039
There is one part of a tiny island called Grímsey,
north of Akureyri, which is where I am now.

00:00:26.039 --> 00:00:30.119
Grímsey is home to fewer than a hundred people
and way more than a hundred puffins.

00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:32.090
And it's a good story.

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:33.090
I was going to travel there,

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:36.260
I was going to see the monument they built
at the Arctic Circle line.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:40.690
But because the earth wobbles on its axis,
the Arctic Circle is moving north.

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:44.910
So there's now a new monument: an eight-tonne
spherical stone boulder

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.460
that they can move every year
as the line moves.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:50.559
In about fifty years, they'll probably have
to move it into the ocean,

00:00:50.559 --> 00:00:55.560
because the Arctic Circle will have left the
island of Grímsey for a few millennia.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:01.190
And Iceland, so my story went, will no longer
have any territory above the Arctic Circle.

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:03.260
Decent story, right?

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:07.390
But then I found that there's another bit
of Iceland above the Arctic Circle,

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:09.770
another 60km or so further north.

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:12.760
A small, uninhabitable bit of rock called
Kolbeinsey.

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:15.820
And that's the sort of technicality I have
to pay attention to,

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:19.210
if I don't want several hundred emails
pointing out that I'm wrong.

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:23.730
And so, the story about Grimsey sort of fell
apart, it didn't work any more.

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:29.390
Except, there's one line on the Wikipedia article
about that small, uninhabitable bit of rock.

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:31.610
"It is subject to rapid wave erosion,

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:34.970
"and is expected to disappear
in the near future,

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:38.180
"probably around the year 2020."

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:41.720
The trouble is, there's no way for a tourist
to get out to Kolbeinsey.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:43.520
It's just a tiny bit of rock.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:46.940
To see if it's still there, you'd have to
charter a plane.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.269
So I chartered a plane!

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:57.610
We're flying out, right now, to the co-ordinates
of where Kolbeinsey might be.

00:01:57.610 --> 00:01:58.730
Or might not be.

00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:03.950
Here's what I know: in the 17th century,
Kolbeinsey was about 700m by 100m,

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:05.630
and about 100m tall.

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:08.620
It was the massive remnant of
an old volcanic eruption.

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:10.638
But it's been steadily, constantly eroded

00:02:10.638 --> 00:02:15.260
by the ocean, by freeze/thaw cycles,
and by sea ice in winter.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:18.739
By 1986, it was only about 40m by 40m,

00:02:18.739 --> 00:02:22.459
and it only reached about 5m above the waves.

00:02:22.459 --> 00:02:25.710
Iceland built a helipad on there to try to
stop it eroding,

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:29.000
but in 2006 the Coast Guard here reported
that the helipad had collapsed.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:33.450
Iceland isn't reinforcing Kolbeinsey any more:
they seem to have abandoned it to the waves.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:36.269
And it might not seem like that matters.

00:02:36.269 --> 00:02:39.599
But tiny places like that can make a difference,

00:02:39.599 --> 00:02:43.599
because countries' territorial limits and
exclusive fishing areas

00:02:43.599 --> 00:02:46.819
are sometimes decided by rocks like that.

00:02:46.819 --> 00:02:48.970
In the mid-20th century, the UK and Iceland

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:53.110
had what the British media dramatically called
the "Cod Wars",

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:55.409
disputes about fishing rights near Iceland.

00:02:55.409 --> 00:03:00.290
And by the way, Iceland won.
The UK conceded.

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:05.359
Now, Kolbeinsey is mentioned in legal documents
about Iceland's territorial limits,

00:03:05.359 --> 00:03:07.989
but I'm also not really sure whether it matters.

00:03:07.989 --> 00:03:10.810
To work out what's going to happen when it
disappears,

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:14.069
you'd need an expert in international maritime
law and, frankly, a psychic,

00:03:14.069 --> 00:03:16.299
because the answer likely is:

00:03:16.299 --> 00:03:19.420
it gets settled by negotiations between governments,

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:23.790
not by some rando in a plane going "hey, your
island isn't there any more!"

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:29.299
But it does feel a bit provocative for me,
as a British person,

00:03:29.299 --> 00:03:33.440
to be checking up on whether Iceland's rock
is still there.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:36.469
All right. We are approaching the co-ordinates.

00:03:36.469 --> 00:03:40.569
We are flying only 500 feet above the waves,
that's about 150m.

00:03:40.569 --> 00:04:03.460
It's low tide. The weather is clear.

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:47.929
If there was ever a chance to see if Kolbeinsey
is still there, this is it.

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:55.010
I think I can see it!

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:59.529
I'm pretty sure that's it up ahead.

00:03:59.529 --> 00:04:03.460
Oh, man, there's barely anything.

00:04:07.989 --> 00:04:13.419
That used to be an island 700m by 100m, and
that's all that's left.

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:19.200
And yes, it's eroded away, but it looks like
predictions were a little bit pessimistic.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.810
There's two little islands still poking above
the water.

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:24.530
Importantly, it is still there,

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:27.840
at low tide it is still above the waves.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:31.340
Iceland, your island is still there.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:33.520
You're welcome!

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:36.720
This video was inspired by some tweets from
Sam Hughes, a brilliant science fiction author.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:38.610
I recommend his books Ra and Fine Structure,

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:42.340
he's also responsible for the Antimemetics
Division stories over on the SCP Wiki.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:44.140
I've linked to his work in the description.

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:45.190
Sam, thank you very much.

